Here's what I've discovered so far :

top and other programs are wrong in some way.... they display the right
values but doesn't really know what's going on behind the scenes.

Your machine is probably running in eager swap mode. This is the default
and makes it allocate swap in advance to make sure there's enough swap
space available for the running programs in case they or their data
needs to be paged out.
This will make it seem like the machine is swapping, and if there's a
lot of activity going on it'll seem like it's swapping alot, but in fact
it might just be allocating alot of swap space it doesn't use.
You can see this with a "swapon -sv" :

# swapon -sv
Swap partition /dev/vol/rootdg/swapvol (default swap):
    Allocated space:       131008 pages (1023MB)
    In-use space:               1 pages (  0%)
    Free space:            131007 pages ( 99%)


Total swap allocation:
    Allocated space:       131008 pages (1023MB)
    Reserved space:          6363 pages (  4%)
    In-use space:               1 pages (  0%)
    Available space:       124645 pages ( 95%)

You can see that 4 percent is reserved, but 0% in actual use. In other
words, this machine doesn't swap but has a 4% allocation.

To be able to fully see the actual swap need, you can change to lazy
swap (set the vm-swap-eager parameter in sysconfigtab to 0) (on 4.0x
remove the /sbin/swapdefault link). This will make it use swap only when
it needs to, but you don't get the security as with eager swap, where
you'll get a failure at program startup if there isn't swap enough. In
lazy mode your program will most likely crash when it request more swap
(memory) than available. In eager mode they'll usually crash on startup,
or if well written, exit nicely with a error message stating that the
requested memory couldn't be allocated.
The OS will at all time log in messages "Unable to obtain requested swap
space" if you hit the limit.
On heavy loaded memory poor systems it can often be a good idea to use
lazy swap anyway... else you'll get a "Swap space below X%" all the time
on both the console and all ptys (I hate the last bit) because stuff
like compilers and other memory-hungry-with-a-short-timespan programs
make the eager swap allocate all the swap.
But on well dimensioned systems, eager swap is usually a "good thing".
If you change to lazy swap, the "Reserved space" field will disappear
when doing a "swapon -sv".


Next thing is the memory usage.
Memory you don't use will automagically be used by the OS for
cache/buffering. If the system is incative, they'll be small; if not and
there's free memory, they'll be large :

# vmstat -P

Total Physical Memory =    80.00 M
                      =    10240 pages


Physical Memory Clusters:

