Gary,

Advanced Printing Software does print landscape, portrait 
and n-up of text files to just about any PostScript printer 
through a JetDirect card.  (Formatting a document in landscape 
or portrait format is usually accomplished with the generating
application to make sure the page is "the right shape".)

If you're still interested in using Advanced Printing, just 
make sure the logical and physical printers have 
 
 content-orientations-supported="portrait landscape" 

set up on them.  If you used the /usr/pd/scripts/pd_get_started 
tool, it should have been set up automatically.  Then print 
with a command like

  > pdpr -x cont-or=landscape <text file>

Similarly, for n-up printing, the pd_get_started script 
sets up numbers-up-supported="0 1 2 4".  So to print a text 
file 2-up, use

  > pdpr -x number-up=2 <text file>

--Rob Hamilton



