 You were'nt clear if you're printing straight text or postscript files, and
if you're using lpd or tcp/ip printing.  We do straight text tcp/ip printing
(reports, etc.).
For that, you do the following:

 1) HP JetDirect receives tcp/ip print jobs on port 9100.  Put an entry in
your
  /etc/services file.

 2) Put an entry in your /etc/hosts file for the printer.

 3) Make an entry in your /etc/printcap file for the portrait printer

 4) Create a file in the /usr/lbin directory that contains the escape
sequence
  to put the printer into landscape.

 5) Create a shell script to concatenate the print job with the escape
  sequence and pipe it to the printer.

 Here's what one of ours looks like:
Printcap entry for portrait:

lp217|217|ATLBIGJOBN_P:\
        :ct=tcp:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/hplaserof:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp216err:\
        :lp=@ATLBIGJOBN/hplaser:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/hplaserof:\
        :pw#80:\
        :pl#55:\
        :rw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd216:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:
# tcp/ip entry for ATLBIGJOBN landscape
lp218|218|ATLBIGJOBN_L:\
        :lp=/dev/null:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/landfil218:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd218:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:

Script file /usr/lbin/landfil218

#! /bin/csh
/bin/cat /usr/lbin/addland - | lpr -h -Plp217

/usr/lbin/addland:
^[(s16.67H ^[&l1O

Note:

 the ^[ are escape's.

This seems to work well for us.

Hope this helps.

