Hello,

I am currently running into problems in the use of the SigC++ library
which boild down to the following short example:

--------------
template <class T> void func(void (T::*f)(int) ) {;}

class Foo {
private:
	class Pimpl {
		void bar(int);
		Pimpl() {
			// this is supposed to be the right way
			func(&Foo::Pimpl::bar);
			// this almost works, but is not standard
			func(&bar);
		}
	};
};
--------------

When compiling this code with digital C++ 6.1-027 in strict_ansi mode, 
I get a warning for the second form (as the standard says), but the 
first one gives me an error:

cxx: Error: bug.C, line 9: class "Foo::Pimpl" is inaccessible
                        func(&Foo::Pimpl::bar);
-----------------------------------^

However, when compiling with gcc 2.95.2 (-Wall -ansi -pedantic) the 
first form is accepted without problem. 

So, is that a bug in this cxx version (and in this case, has it been fixed 
since then) or should the example program be changed to comply with
the standard?

Thanks in advance for any help.
JMarc

