If you're running UNIX V4.0D or later, you should look
at man protect_headers_setup(8). This script will "protect"
the system header files (and 64-bit interfaces) from the 
flags that alter the pointer size (such as -xtaso_short).

There are also some online docs that you can read:

http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/faqs/publications/pub_page/doc_list.html

You may want to check out the Programmer's Guide. Below is the
(long) URL to the one for UNIX V5.0A, for example:

http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/V50A_HTML/ARH9VBTE/TITLE.HTM

Appendix A describes "Using 32-Bit Pointers on Tru64 UNIX
Systems"

As you noted, the best alternative is to clean up the code
so that you don't need to use these workarounds.

Hope it helps.

-Jeff



