You can probably "get by" with just using the linker's "-taso" option. This
restricts addresses returned by system calls to the lower 31 bits. If you
cast a pointer to an int and then back again, things should be ok as it will
have just chopped off 0's in the high order bytes. It's not pretty, but it
works. In your code, pointers should still be allocated as 8 byte quantities
however. A "long" is always 8 bytes, no matter what. There are no options to
change their size.

In my experience, using the -xtaso and -xtaso_short compiler options and
#pragma pointer_size are usually more trouble than it is worth. I steer
clear of them.

Jeff Sullivan gave some good suggestions on using the latest compiler and
it's diagnostics to clean up all your 64 bit issues. You can also use the
Porting Assistant as well as "lint" and some of it's options like "-Q".

Regards,
Jeff Donsbach



