1) I don't tend to defragment my advfs filesystems and they don't seem to get much
slower but I have no measurements to back that up. However...
You can defragment advfs filesystems with the defragment(8) command.
You can set a time limit on how long you allow the process to run so the part
about how long it takes is up to you.
Before you can defragment a file domain, all filesets in the file domain
must be mounted. If you try to defragment an active file domain that
includes unmounted filesets, the system displays an error message
indicating that a fileset is unmounted.

2) Probably, although the only real problem I've had with advfs is that backing
it up over a network with vdump can be a pain. I'm told that V5 fixes this.

3) No you can use advfs filesets for any part of the filesystem including /

Hope this helps
Adam Price







